      "international_printing_museum_carson_c_2": "International Printing Museum (Carson, CA) — Working Gutenberg replica + tours",
      "international_printing_museum_carson_c_3": "International Printing Museum (Carson, CA) runs Gutenberg demonstrations on real reconstructions. Gutenberg Museum (Mainz) has originals. Neither requires special arrangement.",
      "internet_archive": "Internet Archive",
      "it_is_1455_in_a_mainz_print_shop_choos": "It is 1455 in a Mainz print shop. Choose a role — apprentice, compositor, pressman, or master printer — and walk through four decisions across a working day. Your choices shape who you become. Pure role-play history.",
      "it_is_fair_to_say_korean_printing_exis": "It is fair to say Korean printing existed first; it is not fair to say it would have done what Gutenberg's did. The Korean tradition served different needs (state, temple, scholarly) in a different social structure. Gutenberg arrived at a moment when European cities had merchant classes, growing literacy, a Latin-reading church-and-university audience, and the social fuel for an information revolution. Same technology in different soil produces different harvests. Korean printing was a parallel invention, not a path not taken.",
      "italic": "Italic",
      "italic_2": "Italic",
      "italic_aldine": "Italic (Aldine)",
      "italic_aldus_manutius_1500": "Italic (Aldus Manutius, ~1500)",
      "italic_was_invented_for_space_not_emph": "Italic was invented for SPACE, not emphasis — to fit more text per line in pocket-sized books.",
      "james_moran_printing_presses_history_a": "James Moran, Printing Presses: History and Development from the Fifteenth Century to Modern Times (1973)",
      "japan": "Japan",
      "japanese_manga_history_formats_major_t": "Japanese manga: history, formats, major titles, global influence, Maine connections.",
      "jean_jacques_rousseau_has_sent_you_mil": "Jean-Jacques Rousseau has sent you \"Émile\" — a treatise on education that includes the Profession of Faith of the Savoyard Vicar, arguing for natural religion outside the Catholic Church. Geneva is Protestant + relatively tolerant, but other European states have condemned the book. The Sorbonne has issued a warrant against Rousseau.",
      "jenson_nicolas_jenson_venice_1470": "Jenson (Nicolas Jenson, Venice 1470)",
      "jeremy_popkin_revolutionary_news_the_p": "Jeremy Popkin, Revolutionary News: The Press in France, 1789-1799 (1990)",
      "jerry_brotton_a_history_of_the_world_i": "Jerry Brotton, A History of the World in Twelve Maps (2012)",
      "jikji": "Jikji",
      "jikji_entry_unesco_memory_of_the_world": "Jikji entry, UNESCO Memory of the World register (2001)",
      "jikji_korea": "Jikji (Korea)",
      "jikji_used_sand_mold_casting_less_repe": "Jikji used sand-mold casting (less repeatable). Gutenberg used the hand-mold + copper matrix (very repeatable). Repeatability matters when you need 50,000 identical sorts.",
      "joad_raymond_ed_the_cambridge_companio": "Joad Raymond, ed., The Cambridge Companion to British Civil War Print (2015)",
      "johann_carolus_had_been_writing_handwr": "Johann Carolus had been writing handwritten newsletters by hand for a small subscription audience (wealthy merchants + diplomats) since the 1590s. By 1604 he had so many subscribers that hand-copying was unsustainable. He bought a press, started printing the newsletter as the Relation, and petitioned the Strasbourg city council in late 1605 for a printing monopoly. The petition (which survives) is the founding document of the newspaper as a form.",
      "johannes_gutenberg_mainz_germany": "Johannes Gutenberg, Mainz, Germany",
      "johnson_1755": "Johnson (1755)",
      "johnson_s_1755_dictionary_webster_oed_": "Johnson's 1755 dictionary → Webster → OED → Merriam-Webster online. How language gets standardized into print.",
      "joseph_moxon_mechanick_exercises_on_th": "Joseph Moxon, Mechanick Exercises on the Whole Art of Printing (1683-84) — primary source on period inks",
      "julia_child_modern": "Julia Child + modern",
      "justified": "Justified",
      "justified_text_every_line_the_same_wid": "Justified text (every line the same width)",
      "katsushika_hokusai_s_the_great_wave_of": "Katsushika Hokusai's \"The Great Wave off Kanagawa\" (1830-1832) was made with around 8 blocks, printed in editions of about 5,000-8,000 over several years. Sold cheaply (about the price of a bowl of noodles), it was mass-market entertainment, not high art. When Japanese ukiyo-e arrived in Europe in the 1850s-60s, they shocked artists like Whistler, Monet, and Van Gogh — sparking japonisme and reshaping Western painting.",
      "kelmscott_doves_officina_bodoni_modern": "Kelmscott → Doves → Officina Bodoni → modern. Limited-edition handpress book art across 130 years.",
      "kelmscott_press": "Kelmscott Press",
