      "turns": "Turns of twist in the bundle",
      "tut1": "Welcome to Machine Lab. Start in the Machine Shop: six simple machines, one idea. Mechanical advantage trades distance for force and never creates energy.",
      "tut1_g35": "Welcome to Machine Lab! Start in the Machine Shop: six simple machines, each one trading distance for force.",
      "tut1_g912": "Welcome to Machine Lab. The Machine Shop establishes the six ideal machines and their mechanical advantages; everything after it is those six composed into a real engine.",
      "tut1_k2": "Welcome to Machine Lab! Start in the Machine Shop. Six simple machines, and every one of them helps you push less hard.",
      "tut2": "Then open Build. A trebuchet, a ballista and an onager are not new machines: the panel there names every part as one of the six you just met, and takes you back to its bench.",
      "tut3": "Watch the energy ledger beside the machine. Follow the joules from the crank to the stone, and see exactly where each one is lost.",
      "tut4": "In the Test Range, predict the distance before you fire. Try a very light stone and a very heavy one: the fastest shot is not the furthest.",
      "tut5": "At the Target Wall, aim flat. A high lob sails clean over a wall; walls are battered with direct fire.",
      "typed_q": "Predict the effort force these settings need, in newtons. Do it before you read the panel above.",
      "vacuum_note": "Turn the air off and sweep the stone mass: the range now falls steadily as mass rises, with no best value. The sweet spot you find with air on is a fact about drag, not about levers.",
      "wall_caption": "Condition of each course, counting up from the ground",
      "wall_curtain": "Curtain wall",
      "wall_curtain_l": "A plain wall. Energy per hit against the block budget, nothing else going on.",
      "wall_gatehouse": "Gatehouse",
      "wall_gatehouse_l": "The arch carries its load sideways into the stones beside the opening. Take one of those away and only that half of the arch comes down.",
      "wall_gl_failed": "3D wall unavailable. The diagram below is the same wall.",
      "wall_gl_loading": "Loading the 3D wall...",
      "wall_imported": "Your own build",
      "wall_imported_l": "This is the castle you built in Architecture Studio, seen face on. Depth counts as strength here: where you stacked the wall thicker, it takes proportionally more energy to break through.",
      "wall_keep": "Keep",
      "wall_keep_l": "Granite, and ten courses of it. Where you aim matters more than how hard you throw.",
      "wall_motte": "Motte and tower",
      "wall_motte_l": "The tower stands on an earth mound. Earth gives way far sooner than stone does, and everything above it follows.",
      "wall_state": "Wall state",
      "watch_motion": "Watch the motion",
      "wedge": {
        "copy_g35": "Divide the wedge length by how thick it is at the back. A long slim wedge has a big number, so it pushes the log apart harder for the same hammer blow.",
        "copy_g68": "MA = length / thickness. A wedge is an inclined plane that moves through the material instead of the load moving along it. The trade is the same: you drive it a long way in to push the halves apart a little.",
        "copy_g912": "The ideal MA is purely geometric, but wedges are the least ideal of the six machines in practice. Friction between the faces and the material usually dominates, and is often what stops the wedge springing back out, so a splitting wedge deliberately trades efficiency for that self-holding behaviour.",
        "copy_k2": "A wedge is a ramp that moves. An axe, a knife and your front teeth are all wedges. Long thin ones push things apart more easily than short fat ones.",
        "k2_a": "A long, thin wedge",
        "k2_b": "A short, fat wedge",
        "k2_c": "Shape does not change anything",
        "k2_q": "Which wedge splits a log more easily?",
        "label": "Wedge",
        "length": "Wedge length",
        "load": "Splitting resistance",
        "thickness": "Wedge thickness"
      },
      "went_over": "Over the top. Lower the release angle or take some energy out.",
      "went_wide": "Wide of the wall by ",
      "went_wide_calm": " m.",
      "went_wide_wind": " m. The crosswind is pushing it sideways; aim off into the wind or wait for it to drop.",
      "what_to_notice": "What to notice",
      "winch_body": "Gearing the winch changes how hard you crank and how many turns it takes. Watch what it does to the shot.",
      "winch_body_g35": "Changing the handle and the drum changes how hard you pull and how many turns it takes. See what it does to the shot, and what it does not do.",
      "winch_body_k2": "The handle and the drum decide how hard you have to pull and how many times you have to turn. Change them and watch what happens to the throw.",
      "winch_drum": "Winch drum radius",
      "winch_handle": "Crank handle radius",
      "winch_hint": "Change the gearing and watch: the first three numbers move, the launch speed does not.",
      "winch_hint_g35": "Change the gearing and watch. The first three numbers move. The launch speed does not, because gearing changes how you do the work, not how much of it there is.",
      "winch_hint_k2": "Try it: the first numbers change, but the launch speed stays the same. The winch decides how easy the work is, not how far the stone goes.",
      "winch_ma2": "Winch mechanical advantage: ",
      "winch_ma_gloss": "That means the winch pulls ",
      "winch_ma_gloss2": " times harder than you do.",
      "winch_pulleys": "Pulleys in the tackle",
      "wind": "Crosswind",
      "wind_g35": "A crosswind blows the stone off to one side. The longer it is in the air, the further it drifts.",
      "wind_g68": "Drag acts on the stone’s speed THROUGH THE AIR, not over the ground, so a crosswind pushes it sideways for the whole flight. Slow, light stones drift most.",
      "wind_g912": "The drag term uses velocity relative to the air mass, so a steady crosswind produces lateral acceleration until the stone matches the air sideways. Drift therefore grows with flight time and falls as the ballistic coefficient rises.",
      "wind_k2": "Wind pushes the stone sideways while it flies.",
      "windlass": {
        "copy_g35": "The handle sweeps a big circle while the drum winds up a small one. Divide the handle radius by the drum radius to see how many times your turn is multiplied.",
        "copy_g68": "MA = R_handle / r_drum. One full turn moves your hand 2 pi R while the rope winds only 2 pi r, so the extra distance is exactly where the extra force comes from.",
        "copy_g912": "This is a continuous lever: the torque balance is F_effort x R = F_load x r. Every rotation restates the same moment equation as the lever bench, which is why the two formulas look identical once you write them as ratios of radii.",
        "copy_k2": "A big wheel turning a small one helps you lift. Think of a doorknob: the round part is wide, so it is easy to turn the skinny bit inside.",
        "drum_r": "Drum radius",
        "handle_r": "Handle radius",
        "k2_a": "A big handle to turn",
        "k2_b": "A tiny handle to turn",
        "k2_c": "Handle size does not matter",
        "k2_q": "You are winding a bucket up from a well. What makes it easiest?",
        "label": "Wheel & Axle",
        "load": "Load"
      },
